---
id: org.dxos.docs
name: DXOS docs
version: 0.1.0
---

The DXOS docs teach a developer to build on ECHO, then to build apps from plugins with PLEX, using
Composer as the app those plugins run in. They have three sections: ECHO; HALO, which holds identity, devices and
space membership; and PLEX, which holds the plugin docs and the tool chain. ECHO comes first, and `/docs/` opens its Overview page;
there is no separate docs home [F-16]. Every section has the same shape: Start here, a learning path that builds one
thing step by step, core concepts, an API group with one page per core namespace, guides, and resources [F-17], [F-18].

This document is the source of truth for what the docs cover and how. Each `page` block gives the page's section,
place in the sidebar, template and status, a one-line summary, and under `covers:` the points the page must cover.
Pages are written, and rewritten when the APIs change, from these blocks [F-5].

The ECHO developer site is the technical reference for building on ECHO, the typed, local-first
object graph. It is for an engineer deciding whether to adopt ECHO, then learning it, then looking
something up. It is part of dxos.org: the landing page is `dxos.org/echo`, a product page beside
`/composer` and `/plugins`, and the written pages are the ECHO section of the dxos.org docs, which ECHO
leads [F-7].

The site is part of the dxos.org Astro build, with Starlight as the documentation shell. Its theme is the
theme of every dxos.org docs page. The landing page is built on the dxos.org Webflow layout, like
`/composer` and `/plugins`, and uses the theme's fonts and code colors inside it. Its look is flat and comes
from the DXOS brand assets: logos, background art, fonts and palette. Its structure follows the Effect documentation: a short
argument for the idea, then a learning path, core concepts, guides and a reference.

The site opens with why ECHO exists: it is the data model that plugins code against, the place agents keep
their memory, a schema an agent can inspect, and the graph where data from external systems meets [F-14].

Three properties outrank everything else:

- **Every code sample compiles against this commit.** The ECHO docs on dxos.org teach APIs that no
  longer exist, so a sample that is not checked is assumed to be wrong [F-3].
- **Every claim about behavior cites a spec item.** A page says what ECHO guarantees only when
  `org.dxos.echo` or `org.dxos.echo.query` states it [F-4].
- **The spec owns the site map.** The sidebar, the planned pages and what each page covers come from the
  `page` blocks in this document [F-5].

## Dependencies

```mdl
uses org.dxos.echo: The public API that the learning path and the reference document.
```

```mdl
uses org.dxos.echo.query: The query guarantees that the argument for ECHO rests on.
```

```mdl
uses org.dxos.app.composer: The app that the PLEX section uses as its host, and teaches the reader to extend.
```

## Extensions

| Term       | URI                                 |
| ---------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `uses`     | `dxos.spec.core/uses`               |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`             |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`         |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`         |
| `question` | `org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0`         |
| `template` | `org.dxos.docs.template@1.0`     |
| `page`     | `org.dxos.docs.page@1.0`         |

## Templates

Each page uses exactly one template. The template fixes the order of the page's parts, so a reader
always knows where to look.

```mdl
template landing: The front door
  purpose: Answer what ECHO is and why it differs, in one screen, then route the reader.
  parts:
    - Hero: eyebrow, one-line claim, one-paragraph mechanism, install chip, two actions, ECHO background art
    - Sample: the smallest complete example
    - Guarantees: four cards, each a behavior with its spec citation
    - Live: the live demos [F-7.5]
    - Architecture: one diagram of the packages
    - Routes: cards into the learning path, concepts and reference
```

```mdl
template narrative: An argument
  purpose: Explain why, in the order of the Effect page "Why Effect?".
  parts:
    - Title and one-paragraph answer
    - The pattern: the smallest complete example and an anatomy strip of its key signature
    - Comparison: the same task written without and with ECHO, side by side
    - Practical problems: one H3 per problem, each with its mechanism and spec citation
    - Closing: where to go next
```

```mdl
template tutorial: A learning path step
  purpose: Teach one skill. The reader ends with code that runs, and sees the piece of the tutorial app it builds.
  parts:
    - Title and a one-sentence outcome
    - Background: the facts the steps rely on, with a link to the concept page for the rest
    - Steps: numbered, each one action, one snippet and one observable result
    - Try it: the tutorial app as it stands after this page, live [F-15.1]
    - Recap: what the reader can now do
```

```mdl
template concept: A core concept
  purpose: Describe one idea completely, for a reader who has finished the learning path.
  parts:
    - Title and a definition in one sentence
    - Model: the idea, with a diagram where the shape matters
    - Behavior: what is guaranteed, with spec citations
    - Limits: what is not guaranteed, and known divergences
```

```mdl
template reference: One module
  purpose: Answer a lookup in one glance. No narrative.
  parts:
    - Title, import line and a one-sentence summary
    - Members table: strict columns of member, signature, summary
    - One H3 per member family: signature, parameters, a compiled example
```

```mdl
template namespace: One API namespace
  purpose: Teach one namespace in a page a reader can read top to bottom, between the learning path and the generated API reference.
  parts:
    - Title, import line and a one-paragraph summary of what the namespace is for
    - Common tasks: one H3 per task, each with a compiled example
    - Members at a glance: a short table of the members a reader uses most, each linking to the API reference
    - Related: the concept pages and learning-path steps that use the namespace
```

```mdl
template stub: A planned page
  purpose: Show the planned scope of a page that is not written yet.
  parts:
    - Title, a planned badge and the page's summary
    - The page's `covers:` list
```

## Site map

The order of these blocks is the order of the sidebar: section, then group, by first appearance. Routes are
absolute. A page with status `later` is in scope but not yet in the sidebar. There is no docs home page:
`/docs/` redirects to ECHO's Overview, the first page of the first section [F-16.5].

## ECHO

### Start here

```mdl
page welcome: Overview
  What ECHO is and how these docs are organized.
  section: ECHO
  route: /docs/echo/overview/
  group: Start here
  template: concept
  status: spike
  covers:
    - What ECHO is in one paragraph: a typed, local-first, peer-to-peer object graph for TypeScript
    - The model in four points: CRDT replication, peers over WebRTC, concurrent writers, offline writes that merge
    - What DXOS is, in two sentences, with ECHO as the lead
    - Spaces hold data and objects are its unit, with links to both concept pages
    - How these docs are organized: the ECHO, HALO and PLEX sections, and in each the learning path, core
      concepts, guides and resources
    - Where to go next: Why ECHO?, the learning path, and PLEX for building apps on ECHO
```
```mdl
page why-echo: Why ECHO?
  Why apps and agents share one data model: agent memory, introspection, external data, then the argument for a typed, local-first object graph.
  section: ECHO
  route: /docs/echo/why-echo/
  group: Start here
  template: narrative
  status: spike
  covers:
    - Agents need memory they can query, and a schema they can inspect
    - External data needs one graph to meet in, and plugins code against that graph
    - The ECHO pattern: the smallest complete example, then the same task without and with ECHO
    - The practical problems ECHO solves, each with its mechanism and a spec citation
    - How DXOS apps differ from SaaS apps, in the comparison table moved from the old docs home
```

```mdl
page echo-build-with-agent: Build with a coding agent
  Hand the ECHO learning path to Claude Code, Codex or Cursor, with a prompt you can edit first.
  section: ECHO
  route: /docs/echo/build-with-a-coding-agent/
  group: Start here
  template: tutorial
  status: spike
  covers:
    - A prompt the reader can edit, prefilled to build the tutorial app by following the learning path
    - Buttons that open the prompt in Claude Code, Codex or Cursor, and one that copies it; each puts a line the reader does not see before their text, which has the agent read `/echo/llms-full.txt` first
    - What the reader does while the agent works, and what they should see at the end
```

